      {
        "id": 128466,
        "name": "Mezike",
        "username": "Mezike",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/m/c6cbf5/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2015-01-20T20:59:57.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Damn the person who didn't nominate <em>The Lego Movie</em> for anything! Maybe Shawn Patterson will win the song award and induce chronic embarrassment in all concerned. The presence of <em>American Sniper</em> in any of these awards is testament to the crappy backstage rigging that goes on with the Oscars, it's nowhere near good enough to be in the nominations for best movie of the year. <em>Nightcrawler</em> was probably the year's best, or <em>The Imitation Game</em>, but I don't think either will be showered with awards.</p><p>I'd like to see Cumberbatch get the Actors gong, just because, well, Cumberbatch, but I think you may be right about Steve Carrell, he's been threatening some proper peer credibility for some time now. Anyhoo, the important ones for me are writing and cinematography as they are the most vital ingredients in a good movie and I cannot personally argue with your choices. I have a feeling that Emmanuel Lubezki for <em>Birdman</em> will get passed over for Robert Yeoman, they do love their sweeping vistas and extreme close ups in the Oscars, but Lubezki's work was technically brilliant and well deserving.</p><p>In fact it seems weird to see <em>Grand Budapest Hotel</em> in the running, I could have sworn that movie was much older and should have been in last years awards, but that film has \"give me <em>all</em> the awards\" written over it even though it was a lazy and meandering effort - almost like Wes Anderson was doing a parody of himself.</p>",
